### They feel loved or delighted when the child seeks them out and joins in shared moments.

14 of 127 posts.

The child chooses a familiar adult for comfort, returns affection, or turns an ordinary routine into shared play. These accounts include feeding smiles around five to six months, an almost-four-month-old touching a face, an 18-month-old running to hug a father, and a nearly-two-year-old guiding a tired parent’s hands to sign book. One account comes from a caregiver spending time with a 20-month-old, rather than an identified parent.

- "He had obviously had enough and sort out a safe space: Me!" [sr006-0161]
- "[…] I wasnt expecting that she would be capable of expressing so much love so soon." [sr062-0085]
- "[…] after months of one sides kisses and hugs, i just melted in a puddle!!" [sr084-0060]
- "[…] My 18 month old son comes up the stairs sees I’m laying down and goes Dada Dada! And comes running to me and gives me a big hug! […]" [sr056-0009]
- "[…] he grabbed both my hands, put them together, and opened them, signing “book” to me himself. […]" [sr084-0103]

**But:** The pleasure can coexist with sadness about growth: "[…] I feel sad that he’s growing so much." [sr024-0108]

