### They can finally do something for themselves while the child plays independently.

3 of 127 posts.

Independent play creates enough breathing room to eat, do laundry or wash dishes after months of carrying or holding the baby. Two babies are around eight months old; another child is 20 months and plays with blocks and Play-Doh. Parents describe relief in concrete moments of reclaimed time.

- "We can put her in her play pen with some toys and she can actually go a good 15-20 minutes by herself without fussing. After that she’ll start to get a little bored and want to be picked up." [sr011-0016]
- "It’s so nice to finally have a bit of time to wash dishes, eat, watch a quick show, etc. without having her strapped to me." [sr011-0017]
- "I have to say a 20 month our little one is finally able to just sit around and play with Play-Doh or build some blocks and I can do laundry!" [sr045-0054]
- "[…] Now at 7.5 months I’m able to do dishes and laundry while he bats at his toys and blows raspberries. YAY!" [sr093-0105]

**But:** The independence has limits: "[…] I mean he definitely still has his moments where he wants to be held and screaming starts if we put him in a stroller." [sr045-0055]

