### They are delighted by new words or signs while trying to work out what the child means.

13 of 127 posts.

They celebrate first words, longer requests and the milk sign, or ask whether a convincing sound has meaning. Reported ages range from three months for apparent words to 18 months and older toddler stages for requests and pronunciation problems. A first dada can feel like a gift, while egg, no or a word sounding obscene creates surprise or awkwardness; the reported early speech is the parent’s interpretation.

- "My 10-month-old daughter had her first word today on Father's day. It was "dada"" [sr051-0128]
- "[…] Definitely his first word. Not mama or dada, but “egg” 😂. […]" [sr089-0010]
- "Right after 11 months the sign for milk clicked and he started doing it every nursing session. Yesterday he signed milk to me while sitting and today he signed it to my husband." [sr129-0007]
- "[…] IN PUBLIC, whenever he sees a truck or a digger, he enthusiastically YELLS “D!CK!” […]" [sr082-0047]
- "[…] he started using a bunch of little sentences - I want it, I no want it, I want go down, I want break out/down - […]" [sr071-0074]

**But:** Speech-like sounds are not established comprehension, and more words can still leave the parent unable to understand: "[…] Even if he said it, there’s no way he can process the meaning of that yet. […]" [sr115-0065] "[…] Cue the waterworks when he's said something 5 times and I'm still not getting it!" [sr071-0075]

