### They are delighted by new words or signs while trying to work out what the child means.

18 of 124 posts.

They enjoy language appearing in longer sentences, creative labels, imitation, jokes, polite persuasion and multilingual exchanges. Many ages are unknown; several are two-year-olds retained in the near section. The range includes a first Papa, an involved father finally being called dada, mispronunciations of snacks and unicorn, calling the mother bro, whispering negotiations, translating for a parent and applying conversations about death to a new dog.

- "Apparently “clacks” means snacks. And more importantly… “crap craps” means “crackers.”" [sr001-0122]
- "[…] now he wakes up and does a full round of good mornings to everything in the house. the sofa. the fan. his toys. the wall. […]" [sr029-0096]
- "My daughter has discovered whispering, but only as a negotation tactic. She will walk up, put one hand beside her mouth like she is sharing state secrets, and whisper, "cookie?" with the seriousness of a tiny diplomat." [sr096-0090]
- "[…] he then looked at me and said the same thing in West Frisian and we were both quite shocked. […]" [sr117-0123]
- "We have since gotten a new dog, and last night he solemnly informed us that the new dog will die too, and when she does, he would like a white dog next." [sr133-0168]

**But:** The expanding vocabulary can still be hard to decode: "[…] You have no idea what they said and have no context for the words, but they do so you just have to say "yeah?" […]" [sr124-0031]

