### They take pride in learning through shared activities, books or teaching.

14 of 124 posts.

Adults enjoy drawing, imaginative play, practical repair, electronics, sport, problem-solving and remembered lessons; most ages are unknown, with a two-year-old drawing in an assessment. Some change how they help, or contrast the child’s confidence with their own upbringing. Two identical Magic Fingers endorsements and a show recommendation are product-focused testimony of unverified provenance; no commercial relationship is established in the share.

- "[…] He was quite proud of himself, and he should be, he did a pretty good job. […]" [sr028-0090]
- "Today was his first game and he had a great time, which is all that matters. […]" [sr028-0096]
- "Today she looked at me and said no mommy, just watch. […]" [sr062-0097]
- "I am proud that she was able to recognize, without any more details, just that people owning other people shouldn't be celebrated." [sr067-0059]
- "[…] His reaction was “oh I just didn’t learn these words yet”. […]" [sr094-0059]

**But:** Helping can mean taking over, and paid training is also part of this range: "[…] I was solving the problem because it made me feel useful, not because she actually needed me to." [sr062-0097] "[…] We’ve put a lot of time and A LOT of money into his training and playing. […]" [sr043-0087]

