### They celebrate a small everyday skill that makes care easier.

5 of 124 posts.

They celebrate independent toothbrushing, potty use, tidying, or fewer overturned bowls. Ages are unknown in these passages, and one compares a helpful toddler with teenagers who do not clean up. The potty account explicitly describes an autistic son, while another parent uses a robot vacuum’s supposed appetite as an incentive to clear the floor.

- "He’s always been good for letting us faff with brushing his teeth. So him taking the toothbrush and doing it himself (half decently too!) was a nice shock." [sr051-0081]
- "It’s our first day potty training my autistic son; he went three times on his own! I’m so proud!" [sr054-0138]
- "[…] when our young toddler stopped treating bowls as drum sets and inverting them every chance he got .. what a red letter day 😂 […]" [sr009-0073]
- "[…] Before I run it, I started telling them that the "Anything left on the floor will get eaten by the vacuum" and magically, nothing left on the floor before bed time lol." [sr082-0067]

**But:** A new habit does not make the children fully independent cleaners: "Of course, it didn’t turn them into little cleaning experts and I still have to remind them sometimes, but it’s been a funny bonus I never expected when I bought it." [sr082-0068]

