### They are proud of new skills but need sleep while the child wants to practise or chat.

13 of 124 posts.

They describe rolling, standing, babbling and new awareness beside short naps, frequent waking, early mornings or split nights. Named ages range from 13 weeks to ten months, alongside a two-year-old singing at 4am. Some seek a fix, while others use humour or a developmental explanation to get through it; one account’s claimed age conflicts with its own timeline and is left unresolved.

- "[…] part of me finds this HILARIOUS and the other part of me is absolutely exhausted." [sr008-0109]
- "[…] I obviously enjoy my 8mo developing and it’s wonderful to see her hit all these milestones, but at the same time my wife and I have been functioning on 4 hours of sleep for the past two weeks" [sr017-0196]
- "[…] What is she even talking about at 3am?" [sr095-0073]
- "Tired. Proud" [sr104-0089]
- "[…] He must has been on a boss level last night, because he spent some serious XP points on that roll-over skill this morning! […]" [sr047-0086]

**But:** Developmental interpretations do not establish the cause of the sleep problem, and the parents acknowledge that: "[…] even if scientifically this is inaccurate, we are using this as part of our coping system […]" [sr047-0086] "Our son still sleeps through the night […]" [sr044-0065]

