### They celebrate progress after fearing that a skill might not come.

6 of 127 posts.

They celebrate a spoken word, hands-and-knees crawling, social smiling, a belly laugh or walking after delay, comparison or difficult medical beginnings. This includes an almost-12-month-old crawler, a preterm baby laughing at 21 weeks/15 corrected, a six-month-old/three-month-adjusted baby, two matching posts about a speech-delayed toddler, and a 17-month-old after surgery. These are six sampled posts, including the two matching speech accounts, rather than six established separate families.

- "It may seem like nothing for most 1-year-olds, but to any other parents dealing with a speech delay this is a huge win!" [sr052-0003]
- "[…] I started to forget about it and (get this) let her develop at her own pace! And then she just randomly did it!" [sr076-0013]
- "[…] It's been a week and I still cry happy tears every time." [sr095-0011]
- "[…] my baby went from not laughing, not even a giggle, to a full on BELLY LAUGH today!!!" [sr102-0034]
- "[…] She’s a perfectly normal toddler, doing all of the things they said she’d never do. […]" [sr132-0086]

**But:** Some accounts involve evaluation or treatment, so progress cannot all be described as simply waiting: "[…] We've gone through the evaluation for speech and are just waiting to schedule our first session, but I've worked SO hard with him on my own." [sr052-0001] "[…] Then she had surgery. I will always owe these doctors everything, because everything changed literally the morning after. […]" [sr132-0085]

