### They enjoy who the child is becoming and grieve the baby stages that are passing.

14 of 127 posts.

New clothes, photographs, a birthday, walking or increasing independence make the passing stage suddenly tangible. Parents want to preserve every version of the child while anticipating conversations and adventures. Ages range from one month and a six-and-a-half-week-old born early to an almost-two-year-old; one approaching first birthday asks primarily what comes next.

- "But there's also this weird grief mixed into it that I wasn't expecting. Every milestone feels like gaining something and losing something at the same time. […]" [sr094-0087]
- "[…] I want him to grow up but I wish I could keep every version of him forever." [sr124-0098]
- "[…] She looks like a little kid! Where did my baby go?!?! I wasn't ready for this!" [sr032-0073]
- "Little guy is about to hit 1 month and already doesn’t fit in the Newborn sleeper we brought him home from the hospital in. No one told me this would make me emotional!" [sr067-0045]
- "[…] My doctor put me on Zoloft, which definitely helps, but doesn’t make the feelings go away quickly." [sr133-0045]

**But:** They also value the new stage, and not every birthday account expresses grief: "[…] I enjoy this more. […]" [sr032-0010] "[…] as challenging as it can be, I'm so happy to be here!" [sr107-0027]

