### They find the child's noises, body discoveries and mischief funny even when they cause trouble.

16 of 127 posts.

They laugh at fake laughter, growls, sighs, screams, rough play, grabbing, hand discovery and messy surprises. The range runs from newborn vocal experiments to a 17-month-old being told not to lick the dog’s bottom. Some interpret the behaviour as deliberate comedy or trolling; others ask whether it is common or simply find the learning process funny.

- "Currently my 11 month old girl will growl like a beast or make my wife laugh by doing a fake “stoner” laugh at her every time my wife stops laughing, causing her to laugh again and then the process repeats ad infinitum." [sr017-0237]
- "Today I caught her… she coughed, saw me panic and then literally started LAUGHING" [sr093-0028]
- "I think mine came this morning when I had to tell my 17mo, “Please do not try to lick to dog’s butt.”" [sr070-0107]
- "[…] Her comedic timing with the sighs is on point." [sr037-0075]
- "[…] I really just find how babies learn to be sooo funny and interesting!" [sr046-0122]

**But:** The funny moment may produce fear or distress, including a baby startled by a repeated noise: "[…] Obviously I panic every single time thinking she’s choking." [sr093-0027] "[…] WELLL she tucked her head into my armpit AGAIN then balled her eyes out. […]" [sr052-0136]

