### They delight in the child's growing will and imitation while navigating the unexpected results.

7 of 127 posts.

They enjoy self-restraint, big emotions, imitation and attempts at independence, sometimes while redirecting them. Examples include a 12-month-old shaking her head at forbidden objects, a 16-month-old replacing hitting with tickling, another attempting a pull-up, and a 22-month-old choosing then fearing a ghost costume. A 23-month-old responds to reduced nursing by asking other people to nurse.

- "[…] She will start to try to pull off my glasses, stop herself and shake her head. […]" [sr020-0169]
- "[…] Tell me why this girl stopped hitting me… and instead started “tickticktickling” my head 😭😭. […]" [sr074-0107]
- "I didn’t realize he was copying me until I was trying to give him half grapes and he reject them and started momming me." [sr102-0112]
- "[…] It wasn’t done very well and his legs were definitely in the wrong holes but I was so shocked. […]" [sr120-0009]

**But:** Finding behaviour fascinating does not mean approving it, and the wished-for costume becomes frightening: "[…] HOWEVER that doesn’t mean I don’t try to correct her or that I praise her for it. […]" [sr048-0029] "[…] “no! I’m too scary!” […]" [sr066-0093]

