### They enjoy a new ability but need to adjust care or manage the worry it brings.

4 of 127 posts.

New awareness, rolling, early cruising or movement-related laughter changes what care requires. A seven-week-old’s cues become hard to interpret, a four-month-old’s rolling prompts practical safety questions, and an almost-seven-month-old’s cruising arrives sooner than wanted. A parent of a five-month-old likes the laughter from bouncing but fears injury.

- "It’s like the world came into focus for the first time, he is overstimulated and forgot how to baby 😫" [sr027-0215]
- "[…] So proud and it’s so fun doing tummy time with him. We are very excited. The only thing is obviously now we need to implement extra safety steps." [sr049-0133]
- "I thought there was more time 😭" [sr042-0022]
- "[…] It makes me so anxious because of SBS. […]" [sr110-0052]

**But:** Enjoyment is still explicit: "[…] But omg she really likes when we’re holding her and we just buckle at the knees really fast or something. […]" [sr110-0052]

