### They want support or room to recover even while enjoying what the child now does.

5 of 124 posts.

They need sleep, practical help, financial security, time for themselves or someone with whom to share successes. Named focal ages are ten weeks, four months, six months and almost one year; the solo working parent’s quoted account describes a two-year-old. Missing support includes a sick partner who normally helps, absent or deceased relatives and no audience for milestones.

- "[…] I’m tying so hard to be a good husband and father but often times I feel like a failure to be honest. […]" [sr022-0084]
- "Don’t get me wrong I love it now that the baby is walking and babbling but girl they walk in two different directions […]" [sr017-0206]
- "So many milestones reached but no one to really tell it to. […]" [sr070-0024]
- "[…] daycare and speech therapist commenting on his amazing imagination and ability to play independently […]" [sr072-0032]

**But:** The lack of help can be temporary rather than an uninvolved partner: "[…] my husband is a very involved father and it shows now that he basically cannot do anything with our baby. […]" [sr034-0051]

